# DeepRead settings.
# Copy this file to .env.local and fill it in: cp .env.example .env.local
# .env.local is never committed. Values already set in the shell win over this file.

# Where your books and notes are kept.
#   local - the data folder on this computer (the default)
#   r2    - a Cloudflare R2 bucket, so the library survives this computer
DEEPREAD_STORAGE=local

# The most space your library may take up. An upload that would go past it is refused.
# Units: KB, MB, GB, TB (1 GB = 1000 MB) or KiB, MiB, GiB, TiB (1 GiB = 1024 MiB).
# Leave it empty for no limit.
DEEPREAD_STORAGE_LIMIT=8GB

# Cloudflare R2, used only when DEEPREAD_STORAGE=r2.
# In the Cloudflare dashboard: R2 > Manage API tokens > Create API token, with "Object Read & Write" on your bucket.
# The page that follows shows the access key id, the secret access key and the S3 endpoint.
# Use the jurisdiction endpoint when the bucket has one, e.g. https://<account_id>.eu.r2.cloudflarestorage.com
DEEPREAD_R2_ENDPOINT=https://<account_id>.r2.cloudflarestorage.com
DEEPREAD_R2_BUCKET=<bucket-name>
DEEPREAD_R2_ACCESS_KEY_ID=<access-key-id>
DEEPREAD_R2_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=<secret-access-key>
# The folder inside the bucket that DeepRead uses, so the bucket can hold other things too.
DEEPREAD_R2_PREFIX=deepread/

# Encryption at rest: the books, notes, covers and profiles in the data folder or the R2 bucket are encrypted with this
# key, so a copy of the folder or the bucket cannot be read without it. Off when not set.
# Make a key with: openssl rand -hex 32
# Losing the key means losing the books: nothing can open them without it, and it cannot be changed later.
# Keep a copy somewhere safe and apart from the books, like a password manager: never in the bucket, never in the data
# folder, never committed.
# Books you already have stay readable when you turn it on; run pnpm storage:encrypt (with DeepRead stopped) to encrypt them too.
# Left commented out here: even an empty line would hide a value set in .env.
# DEEPREAD_ENCRYPTION_KEY=<64 hex characters>

# Profiles, so several people can share one DeepRead, each with their own books, notes and place in every book.
# Without ADMIN_PASSKEY there is one library that opens without signing in.
# Set it to turn profiles on: everyone then picks their profile and types its code.
# The admin's code is ADMIN_PASSKEY itself, and the admin adds everyone else's profile and code from the admin page.
# The first start with profiles makes the admin's profile and moves the books you already have into it.
# Choose a long passkey, 12 characters or more, because it opens every profile.
# Changing it later signs everyone out.
# Left commented out here: even an empty line would hide a value set in .env.
# ADMIN_PASSKEY=<a long passkey>
# The admin profile's name when it is first made (Admin if not set). The admin can rename it afterwards.
# ADMIN_NAME=Admin

# API model, through OpenRouter: explanations by an API call to an AI model, on your own OpenRouter key.
# It is the helper you can give to readers who have no Claude Code or Codex of their own: with profiles on, each reader
# gets it only when the admin switches it on for them. It uses nobody's Claude Code or Codex sign-in.
# With profiles on, the admin page can set the key, the model and the daily limit too, and what is saved there wins.
# Make a key at https://openrouter.ai/keys and pick a model from https://openrouter.ai/models (its name looks like vendor/model-name).
# OPENROUTER_API_KEY=sk-or-...
# OPENROUTER_MODEL=vendor/model-name
# The most requests one reader may make of it in a day. 0 for no limit; the admin's own are never counted. Default 100.
# OPENROUTER_DAILY_LIMIT=100
